research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7534:          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and let $x, y \\in F$, with\n$|x|$ the absolute value ([[def-abs-value]]). Then\n\n$$|x| \\ge 0; \\quad |x| = 0 \\iff x = 0; \\quad -|x| \\le x \\le |x|; \\quad |-x| = |x|; \\quad |xy| = |x|\\,|y|;$$\n\nand, for every $c > 0$, one has $|x| < c \\iff -c < x < c$.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7545:          "quote": "In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7567:          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). The **absolute value** of\n$x \\in F$ is\n\n$$|x| \\;:=\\; \\begin{cases} x & \\text{if } x \\ge 0, \\\\ -x & \\text{if } x < 0. \\end{cases}$$",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7589:          "quote": "In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7598:          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7607:          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7616:          "quote": "An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7679:          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],\n[[def-real-numbers]]) with its order and absolute value ([[def-real-order]]), and\n$\\mathbb{N}$ is the set of natural numbers with its order ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[def-nat-order]]).\n\nA **sequence of reals** is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. We write\n$x_k$ for the value $x(k)$, call it the **$k$-th term**, and write $(x_k)$, or\n$(x_k)_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}}$, for the function itself. The **range** of $(x_k)$ is\nthe subset $\\{\\, x_k : k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\,\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nLet $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals and let $P$ be a property of indices.\n\n- $(x_k)$ is **bounded** if there is $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \\le M$ for\n  every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$; it is **unbounded** otherwise.\n- $P$ holds **eventually** if there is $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $P(k)$ holds\n  for every $k \\ge K$.\n- $P$ holds **frequently** if for every $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ there is some\n  $k \\ge K$ for which $P(k)$ holds.\n- For $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the **$K$-th tail** of $(x_k)$ is the sequence\n  $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j + K}$ for $j \\in \\mathbb{N}$; it is\n  again a sequence of reals.\n- A function $n : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ is **strictly increasing** if\n  $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$. For such an $n$, the **subsequence** of $(x_k)$\n  along $n$ is the composite $x \\circ n$, written $(x_{n_k})_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}}$;\n  it is again a sequence of reals.\n\n**Convergence and Cauchyness are not defined here.** They are already fixed, for\nsequences of reals, by the published [[def-real-limit]]: $(x_k)$ *converges* to\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for every rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is\n$K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \\varepsilon$ for all $k \\ge K$, and $(x_k)$\nis *Cauchy* when for every rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$\nwith $|x_k - x_l| < \\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \\ge K$. This page builds the\ntoolkit for those two notions and does not restate them. A sequence\n**converges** if it converges to some real, and **diverges** if it does not.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-refuter-refute-r3-subsequences-fs.log:7697:          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],\n[[def-real-numbers]]) with its order and absolute value ([[def-real-order]]), and\n$\\mathbb{N}$ is the set of natural numbers with its order ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[def-nat-order]]).\n\nA **sequence of reals** is a function $x : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$. We write\n$x_k$ for the value $x(k)$, call it the **$k$-th term**, and write $(x_k)$, or\n$(x_k)_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}}$, for the function itself. The **range** of $(x_k)$ is\nthe subset $\\{\\, x_k : k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\,\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nLet $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals and let $P$ be a property of indices.\n\n- $(x_k)$ is **bounded** if there is $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \\le M$ for\n  every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$; it is **unbounded** otherwise.\n- $P$ holds **eventually** if there is $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $P(k)$ holds\n  for every $k \\ge K$.\n- $P$ holds **frequently** if for every $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ there is some\n  $k \\ge K$ for which $P(k)$ holds.\n- For $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the **$K$-th tail** of $(x_k)$ is the sequence\n  $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j + K}$ for $j \\in \\mathbb{N}$; it is\n  again a sequence of reals.\n- A function $n : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ is **strictly increasing** if\n  $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$. For such an $n$, the **subsequence** of $(x_k)$\n  along $n$ is the composite $x \\circ n$, written $(x_{n_k})_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}}$;\n  it is again a sequence of reals.\n\n**Convergence and Cauchyness are not defined here.** They are already fixed, for\nsequences of reals, by the published [[def-real-limit]]: $(x_k)$ *converges* to\n$x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for every rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is\n$K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \\varepsilon$ for all $k \\ge K$, and $(x_k)$\nis *Cauchy* when for every rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$\nwith $|x_k - x_l| < \\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \\ge K$. This page builds the\ntoolkit for those two notions and does not restate them. A sequence\n**converges** if it converges to some real, and **diverges** if it does not.",
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round4.log:16:The relevant on-disk target clauses, quoted with mathematical markup rendered as plain text, are: `def-countable`, “A is at most countable if it is finite or countably infinite”; `thm-rationals-countable`, “the rationals are countably infinite”; `thm-r-uncountable`, “Let R be a complete ordered field. Then R is uncountable”; `lem-subset-of-countable`, “Let A be at most countable and let B ⊆ A. Then B is at most countable”; `thm-product-of-countable`, “If A and B are at most countable then so is A × B”; and `lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n`, “Let A be a nonempty set. Then A is at most countable if and only if there is a surjection s from N to A. Moreover, from any such surjection an injection from A to N is obtained explicitly.”
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round4.log:18:The order and density route is also licensed: `thm-reals-ordered-field` says, “The relation of def-real-order is well defined and makes R a totally ordered field”; `cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete` says, “The Cauchy-sequence reals have the least-upper-bound property”; `def-complete-ordered-field` defines completeness by least upper bounds for every nonempty bounded-above subset; `thm-of-archimedean` says, “Every complete ordered field F is Archimedean”; `def-canonical-natural` says that iota of n is the canonical natural and is also written n times the field unit; and `lem-of-q-dense` says, “For any x less than y in F there is a rational q with x less than iota of q less than y.” The completeness hypothesis in the uncountability theorem is therefore discharged by [L3].
