research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round4.log:5707:[L3] The order of [[def-real-order]] makes $\mathbb{R}$ a totally ordered field ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]) with the least-upper-bound property ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), hence a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]); for every real $t$ there is therefore $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-canonical-natural]]); and for reals $c<d$ there is a rational strictly between them ([[lem-of-q-dense]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round4.log:5764:The relevant on-disk target clauses, quoted with mathematical markup rendered as plain text, are: `def-countable`, “A is at most countable if it is finite or countably infinite”; `thm-rationals-countable`, “the rationals are countably infinite”; `thm-r-uncountable`, “Let R be a complete ordered field. Then R is uncountable”; `lem-subset-of-countable`, “Let A be at most countable and let B ⊆ A. Then B is at most countable”; `thm-product-of-countable`, “If A and B are at most countable then so is A × B”; and `lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n`, “Let A be a nonempty set. Then A is at most countable if and only if there is a surjection s from N to A. Moreover, from any such surjection an injection from A to N is obtained explicitly.”
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round4.log:5766:The order and density route is also licensed: `thm-reals-ordered-field` says, “The relation of def-real-order is well defined and makes R a totally ordered field”; `cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete` says, “The Cauchy-sequence reals have the least-upper-bound property”; `def-complete-ordered-field` defines completeness by least upper bounds for every nonempty bounded-above subset; `thm-of-archimedean` says, “Every complete ordered field F is Archimedean”; `def-canonical-natural` says that iota of n is the canonical natural and is also written n times the field unit; and `lem-of-q-dense` says, “For any x less than y in F there is a rational q with x less than iota of q less than y.” The completeness hypothesis in the uncountability theorem is therefore discharged by [L3].
