research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:36:CURRENT TEXT READ: "[L1] The order of [[def-real-order]] makes $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-real-numbers]]) a totally ordered field ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]) with the least-upper-bound property ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), hence a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and hence Archimedean ([[thm-of-archimedean]]); and $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the unique order-preserving field embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]])."
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:38:ASSESSMENT: Correct. `def-real-order` defines the displayed order by “$x \le y$ iff $x=y$ or $y-x$ is positive.” `def-real-numbers` says: “The real numbers are the quotient ring $\mathbb{R}:=\mathcal C/\mathcal N$.” The proof-bearing targets now supply the missing properties: `thm-reals-ordered-field` says verbatim, “The relation of `def-real-order` is well defined and makes $\mathbb{R}$ … a totally ordered field”; `cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete` says, “The Cauchy-sequence reals $\mathbb{R}_C$ have the least-upper-bound property”; and `def-complete-ordered-field` defines completeness by exactly that property. `thm-of-archimedean` says: “Every complete ordered field … is Archimedean.” Finally, `lem-of-q-embeds` says there is a unique field homomorphism from the rationals and that it “is injective and order-preserving.” The whole chain in [L1] is therefore licensed.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:48:ASSESSMENT: The repaired Remark is true. Step 2.3 explicitly verifies that $[x,x+1)\times[-x,-x+1)$ meets the antidiagonal only in $(x,-x)$. The item must nevertheless be refused because [L3] still says, “The order of $\mathbb{R}$ is total ([[def-real-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]])” and then applies the Archimedean and density theorems without citing any result proving that the constructed reals are a complete totally ordered field. `def-real-order` merely defines the relation. `def-complete-ordered-field` begins conditionally, “Let $F$ be an ordered field,” and defines completeness; it does not prove that $\mathbb{R}$ satisfies the hypothesis. `thm-of-archimedean` is likewise conditional: “Every complete ordered field $F$ is Archimedean.” The missing premises are precisely the conclusions of `thm-reals-ordered-field` and `cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete`, neither of which is in `deps` or cited here.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:56:CURRENT TEXT READ: "[L8] The order of [[def-real-order]] makes $\mathbb{R}$ a totally ordered field ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]), so no real satisfies $s < s$, and $s < t$ together with $t \le s$ is impossible ([[def-complete-ordered-field]])."
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:58:ASSESSMENT: Correct. `def-real-order` gives the relation being discussed. `thm-reals-ordered-field` says verbatim: “The relation of `def-real-order` is well defined and makes $\mathbb{R}$ … a totally ordered field.” The two impossibility claims are immediate consequences of a total order. `def-complete-ordered-field` supplies the ordered-field terminology; the actual proof-bearing citation is now the theorem, as required. The result is used correctly in the extreme-value argument.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/certifier-certify-round2.log:88:ASSESSMENT: Correct. `def-real-limit` says verbatim: “A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals converges to $x$ when for every rational $\varepsilon>0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k-x|<\hat\varepsilon$ for all $k\ge K$. It is Cauchy when for every rational $\varepsilon>0$ there is $K$ with $|x_k-x_l|<\hat\varepsilon$ for all $k,l\ge K$.” Thus [L1] faithfully states both clauses. `def-real-numbers` says that the reals are the quotient of the ring of Cauchy sequences by the ideal of null sequences, while `thm-reals-cauchy-complete` says: “Every Cauchy sequence of real numbers converges to a real number.” `thm-uniqueness-complete-ordered-field` says: “Any two complete ordered fields … are isomorphic via a unique ordered-field isomorphism.” The current next page indeed proves the least-upper-bound route after monotone convergence and Bolzano-Weierstrass, so the two Remarks now distinguish the two available proofs without contradiction.
