research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8715:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:45:1. **Discrete.** $(X, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{disc}})$ is compact if and only if $X$
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8716:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:46:   is finite ([[def-countable]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8717:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:47:2. **Indiscrete.** $(X, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}})$ is compact, for every $X$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8718:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:48:3. **Cofinite.** $(X, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}})$ is compact, for every $X$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8719:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:49:4. **Particular point.** For $p \in X$, the space $(X, \mathcal{T}_p)$ is compact
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8720:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:50:   if and only if $X$ is finite.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8721:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:51:5. **Cocountable.** $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}})$ is neither compact
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8722:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:52:   nor countably compact ([[def-compactness-variants]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8723:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:53:6. **Sierpinski.** Sierpinski space $S = \{a,b\}$ is compact, being finite, and
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8724:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:54:   its subset $\{b\}$ is a compact subset that is not closed
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8725:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:55:   ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8726:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:56:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8727:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:57:Claims 2 and 3 are the ones worth noticing: compactness on its own is a very weak
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8728:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:58:condition, and a space can be compact while separating no two of its points at
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8729:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:59:all.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8730:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:60:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8731:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:61:## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8732:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:62:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8733:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:63:**Given:** A set $X$, a point $p \in X$ where the particular-point topology is in play, and the six topologies of [[def-standard-topologies]].
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8734:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:64:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8735:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:65:[L1] A space is compact when every family of open sets with union the space has a finite subfamily with union the space; a family is finite when it is empty or listable; every space listed as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-topological-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8736:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:66:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8737:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:67:[L2] $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{disc}} = \mathcal{P}(X)$; $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}} = \{\varnothing, X\}$; $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$ consists of $\varnothing$ and the sets with finite complement; $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$ of $\varnothing$ and the sets with at most countable complement; $\mathcal{T}_p$ of $\varnothing$ and the sets containing $p$; and $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}} = \{\varnothing, \{b\}, S\}$ on $S = \{a,b\}$ ([[def-standard-topologies]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8738:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:68:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8739:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:69:[L3] A set is finite when it is equinumerous with a natural number, equivalently when it is listable as $\{x_0, \dots, x_n\}$ or empty; a subset of an at most countable set is at most countable; $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable ([[def-countable]], [[lem-subset-of-countable]], [[thm-r-uncountable]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8740:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:70:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8741:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:71:[L4] A subset $A$ is a compact subset when the subspace it carries is compact, and equivalently when every family of ambient open sets covering $A$ has finitely many members covering it ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]], [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8742:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:72:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8743:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:73:[L5] In a Hausdorff space every compact subset is closed ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], claim 3; [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8744:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:74:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8745:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:75:[L6] The naturals embed in $\mathbb{R}$ by the canonical natural $\iota$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-natural-numbers]]); $\iota$ is injective, which is a lemma about ordered fields and not part of the definition ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), so $\{\iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is a countably infinite subset of $\mathbb{R}$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8746:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:76:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8747:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:77:## Verification
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8748:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:78:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8749:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:79:**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8750:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:80:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8751:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:81:1.1 Claim 1. If $X$ is finite it is compact by [L1], whatever its topology. If $X$ is compact in the discrete topology, the family of singletons $\{\, \{x\} : x \in X \,\}$ is an open cover by [L2], so finitely many singletons cover $X$ and $X$ is listable, hence finite by [L3]. [L1, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8752:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:82:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8753:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:83:1.2 Claim 2. Let $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{ind}}$ have union $X$. If $X = \varnothing$ the empty subfamily covers it; otherwise some member is nonempty, hence equals $X$ by [L2], and that single member is a finite subcover. [L1, L2]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8754:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:84:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8755:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:85:1.3 Claim 4. If $X$ is finite it is compact by [L1]. If $X$ is compact in $\mathcal{T}_p$, then $\{\, \{p,x\} : x \in X \,\}$ is a family of open sets by [L2] with union $X$, so finitely many of its members cover $X$; their union is a listable set, so $X$ is finite by [L3]. [L1, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8756:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:86:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8757:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:87:1.4 Claim 6. $S$ is finite, hence compact by [L1]; the subspace $\{b\}$ is a one-point space and so compact by [L1], making $\{b\}$ a compact subset by [L4]; and $\{b\}$ is not closed, its complement $\{a\}$ not lying in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{Sier}}$ by [L2]. By [L5] this forces $S$ not to be Hausdorff, which it is not: the only open set containing $a$ is $S$. [L1, L2, L4, L5]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8758:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:88:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8759:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:89:2.1 Claim 3. Let $\mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cof}}$ have union $X \ne \varnothing$, the empty case being as in step 1.2. Some $U_0 \in \mathcal{U}$ is nonempty, so $X \setminus U_0$ is finite by [L2], say $X \setminus U_0 = \{y_0, \dots, y_k\}$ or empty; in the second case $\{U_0\}$ covers $X$, and in the first each $y_j$ lies in some member of $\mathcal{U}$, and finitely many members named in this way together with $U_0$ cover $X$. [L1, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8760:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:90:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8761:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:91:2.2 Claim 5. Put $a_n := \iota(n)$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $A_n := \{\, a_m : m > n \,\}$, an at most countable subset of $\mathbb{R}$ by [L6] and [L3], so that $U_n := \mathbb{R} \setminus A_n$ lies in $\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}}$ by [L2]. Every real lies in some $U_n$: a real that is no $a_m$ lies in $U_0$, and $a_m$ lies in $U_m$. So $\{\, U_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$ is an at most countable open cover of $\mathbb{R}$. [L2, L3, L6, step 1.1]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8762:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:92:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8763:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:93:3.1 The sets $U_n$ increase with $n$, since the $A_n$ decrease, so the union of finitely many of them is a single $U_N$, and $a_{N+1} \in A_N$ lies outside it. Hence this at most countable open cover has no finite subcover and $(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{coc}})$ is neither countably compact nor compact, which is claim 5. [L1, L3, step 2.2] ∎
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8764:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:94:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8765:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:95:## Remarks
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8766:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:96:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8767:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:97:**Compactness alone separates nothing.** The cofinite topology on an infinite set is compact and has the property that any two nonempty open sets meet, so it is as far from Hausdorff as a topology can be; the indiscrete topology is compact and has only two open sets. Every theorem on the companion page that concludes something interesting from compactness carries a Hausdorff hypothesis for exactly this reason.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8768:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:98:
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8769:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:99:**The discrete and the cocountable cases fail for different reasons.** A discrete space fails compactness because its singletons already form a cover with nothing to thin; the cocountable topology on $\mathbb{R}$ fails it because countably many points can be shaved off one at a time and no finite stage removes them all. The second failure is at the countable level, which is why it kills countable compactness too.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8863:items/ex-one-point-compactifications-of-the-line-and-of-the-naturals.md:68:[L3] In the discrete topology every subset is open and closed, every subspace is discrete, and a discrete space is compact exactly when it is finite ([[def-standard-topologies]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]], [[ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies]], claim 1; [[def-compact-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8873:items/ex-one-point-compactifications-of-the-line-and-of-the-naturals.md:78:[L8] For every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a natural $m \ge 1$ with $1/m < \varepsilon$, and $\mathbb{R}$ is an ordered field ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], [[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-interval]], [[def-topological-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:10281:research/audit/wave2-real-analysis.proof-contracts.json:1:{"version":1,"level":"published-audit-wave2-real-analysis","scope":["lem-sup-unique","lem-reflection","thm-infimum-property","lem-sup-epsilon","lem-inf-epsilon","lem-max-is-sup","lem-finite-set-has-max","lem-sup-monotone","lem-sup-translate","lem-sup-scale","lem-sup-sum","fs-sup-belongs-to-set","fs-every-set-has-sup","ex-sup-of-open-interval","ex-sup-of-closed-interval","ex-inf-of-reciprocals","ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two","ex-sup-of-sum-of-sets","cex-sup-not-attained","cex-unbounded-set-has-no-sup","cex-empty-set-has-no-sup"],"contracts":{"lem-sup-unique":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","2.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["1.2","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Assume $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of $S$; in particular each of them is an upper bound of $S$ and each is $\\le$ every upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Assume $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$ are both greatest lower bounds of $S$; in particular each of them is a lower bound of $S$ and each is $\\ge$ every lower bound of $S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Applying the leastness of $u_1$ to the upper bound $u_2$ gives $u_1 \\le u_2$, and applying the leastness of $u_2$ to the upper bound $u_1$ gives $u_2 \\le u_1$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Applying the greatestness of $\\ell_1$ to the lower bound $\\ell_2$ gives $\\ell_2 \\le \\ell_1$, and applying the greatestness of $\\ell_2$ to the lower bound $\\ell_1$ gives $\\ell_1 \\le \\ell_2$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"By antisymmetry $u_1 = u_2$ and $\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$, so a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound of $S$ are each unique when they exist, which is what licenses writing $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","L3"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement allows arbitrary S, including the empty set; steps 2.1 and 2.2 use only conditional leastness/greatestness, so uniqueness remains valid."},{"case":"zero","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No zero index, zero divisor, or zero parameter boundary occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 covers the equality collapse forced by the two opposite inequalities."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-reflection":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["3.1","3.2","3.3","3.4","4.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-inverse-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) the additive identity, the multiplicative\nidentity, each additive inverse, and each multiplicative inverse are unique.\nHence the notations $0$, $1$, $-x$, and $x^{-1}$ denote well-defined elements, as\nthe field definition and its consequences assume.","uses":["1.1","1.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"For all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ the field identity $y - x = (-x) - (-y)$ holds, and $-(-x) = x$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The map $s \\mapsto -s$ sends $S$ onto $-S$ and $-S$ onto $-(-S)$, and since $-(-s) = s$ it is a bijection of $S$ with $-S$ whose inverse is itself; hence $-(-S) = S$, and $S$ is nonempty exactly when $-S$ is nonempty.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"For all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$: $x < y$ holds exactly when $y - x$ is positive, which by 1.1 is exactly when $(-x) - (-y)$ is positive, which is exactly $-y < -x$; and $x = y$ holds exactly when $-x = -y$; hence $x \\le y$ if and only if $-y \\le -x$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Suppose $u$ is an upper bound of $S$. Every element of $-S$ has the form $-s$ with $s \\in S$, and $s \\le u$ gives $-u \\le -s$; hence $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"Conversely, suppose $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $-s \\in -S$, so $-u \\le -s$, and applying 2.1 to this inequality gives $s \\le u$; hence $u$ is an upper bound of $S$. This together with 3.1 proves claim 1.","step":"3.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2","step 3.1"]},{"id":"step-3-3","claim":"Suppose $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $\\ell \\le s$, hence $-s \\le -\\ell$, and every element of $-S$ is such a $-s$; hence $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$.","step":"3.3","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-4","claim":"Conversely, suppose $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $-s \\in -S$, so $-s \\le -\\ell$, and applying 2.1 gives $\\ell \\le s$; hence $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$. This together with 3.3 proves claim 2.","step":"3.4","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2","step 3.3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Claim 1 says the upper bounds of $S$ are exactly the negatives of the lower bounds of $-S$, so $S$ is bounded above exactly when $-S$ is bounded below; claim 2 says likewise that $S$ is bounded below exactly when $-S$ is bounded above; combining the two, $S$ is bounded exactly when $-S$ is bounded, and $-(-S) = S$ with $S$ nonempty exactly when $-S$ is nonempty.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","step 3.3","step 3.4","step 1.2","L2"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 explicitly proves nonemptiness is preserved and the involution also sends the empty set to itself."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 explicitly handle double negation and reflection through zero."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 proves the reflection map is its own inverse, including fixed point zero."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.1 and 3.3 prove the two forward bound implications."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.2 and 3.4 prove the two reverse bound implications."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"thm-infimum-property":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["3.1","4.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-reflection","source_section":"Statement","quote":"For $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ write $-S := \\{-s : s \\in S\\}$. Then $-(-S) = S$,\nand $S$ is nonempty if and only if $-S$ is nonempty. Moreover, for all\n$u, \\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ if and only if $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$;\n2. $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ if and only if $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$.\n\nConsequently $S$ is bounded above if and only if $-S$ is bounded below, $S$ is\nbounded below if and only if $-S$ is bounded above, and $S$ is bounded if and\nonly if $-S$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]).","uses":["1.2","2.1","5.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["6.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-sup-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. If $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of\n$S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), then $u_1 = u_2$. If $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$\nare both greatest lower bounds of $S$ ([[def-infimum]]), then\n$\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$.\n\nConsequently a set has at most one supremum and at most one infimum, and the\nnotations $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$ denote single, well-determined real numbers\nwhenever they exist.","uses":["3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["5.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-inverse-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) the additive identity, the multiplicative\nidentity, each additive inverse, and each multiplicative inverse are unique.\nHence the notations $0$, $1$, $-x$, and $x^{-1}$ denote well-defined elements, as\nthe field definition and its consequences assume.","uses":["5.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["5.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"By hypothesis $S \\ne \\emptyset$ and $S$ is bounded below; fix a lower bound $\\ell_0$ of $S$, so $\\ell_0 \\le s$ for every $s \\in S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","choose"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Let $\\ell'$ be an arbitrary lower bound of $S$; then $-\\ell'$ is an upper bound of $-S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Since $S$ is nonempty, so is $-S$, and since $\\ell_0$ is a lower bound of $S$, its negative $-\\ell_0$ is an upper bound of $-S$; hence $-S$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ that is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"By the least-upper-bound property, $-S$ has a least upper bound in $\\mathbb{R}$; write $u := \\sup(-S)$, which is well defined by uniqueness.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","L1","L4"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Define $\\ell := -u$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","construct"]},{"id":"step-4-2","claim":"The element $u$ is the least of the upper bounds of $-S$ and $-\\ell'$ is one of them, hence $u \\le -\\ell'$.","step":"4.2","inputs":["step 1.2","step 3.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Apply the reflection fact to the set $-S$: since $u$ is an upper bound of $-S$, its negative $-u$ is a lower bound of $-(-S)$, and $-(-S) = S$; so $\\ell = -u$ is a lower bound of $S$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 3.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-5-2","claim":"Negating the inequality $u \\le -\\ell'$ reverses it, giving $-(-\\ell') \\le -u$, that is $\\ell' \\le \\ell$.","step":"5.2","inputs":["step 4.2","step 4.1","L5"]},{"id":"step-6-1","claim":"Thus $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ satisfying $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$, so $\\ell$ is a greatest lower bound of $S$; it is the only one, so $\\inf S$ exists and $\\inf S = \\ell = -\\sup(-S)$.","step":"6.1","inputs":["step 5.1","step 5.2","L3","L4"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement excludes the empty set, and step 1.1 anchors the nonempty hypothesis before any existence argument."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 defines the reflected candidate through additive zero and steps 5.1-5.2 verify it."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No equality-collapse or other degenerate parameter is excluded or used in the statement or proof."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 chooses a lower bound only after bounded-below existence is given, and step 3.1 uses completeness only after step 2.1 proves -S nonempty and bounded above."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-epsilon":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper boun
