    39	## Facts & Assumptions
    40	
    41	**Given:** Reals $x, y$ with representatives $(a_n), (b_n)$.
    42	
    43	[L1] A sequence $(u_n)_{n \ge 1}$ of rational numbers is null if, for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$, there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $|u_n| < \varepsilon$ for every $n \ge N$ ([[def-null-sequence]]).
    44	
    45	[L2] Ordered-field arithmetic in $\mathbb{Q}$: $\delta/2 > 0$; sums and products of eventual lower bounds ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]]).
    46	
    47	[L3] Dichotomy for non-null Cauchy sequences: eventually $> \delta$ or eventually $< -\delta$ ([[lem-cauchy-away-from-zero]]).
    48	
    49	[L4] $\mathbb{R}$ is a field ([[thm-reals-field]]).
    50	
    51	[L5] In $\mathbb{R} = \mathcal{C}/\mathcal{N}$, $x = 0$ iff a representative is null; so $x \ne 0$ iff every representative is non-null ([[def-real-numbers]]).
    52	
    53	## Proof
    54	
    55	**Proof technique:** direct.
    56	
    57	1.1 Positivity is independent of the representative: if $a_n > \delta$ for $n \ge N$ and $(a'_n - a_n)$ is null, then beyond some $N' \ge N$ also $|a'_n - a_n| < \delta/2$, so $a'_n > \delta/2$: the defining property holds for $(a'_n)$ with $\delta/2$. [L1, L2]
    58	
    59	1.2 Trichotomy: if $x \ne 0$, any representative is non-null, so by the dichotomy either $a_n > \delta$ eventually ($x$ positive) or $a_n < -\delta$ eventually ($-x$ positive); the two exclude each other, and exactly one of $x$ positive, $x = 0$, $-x$ positive holds. [L1, L3, L5]
    60	
    61	1.3 Positives are closed under $+$ and $\cdot$: from $a_n > \delta$ and $b_n > \delta'$ eventually, $a_n + b_n > \delta + \delta'$ and $a_n b_n > \delta\delta'$ eventually, with $\delta + \delta', \delta\delta' > 0$. [L2]
    62	
    63	2.1 Consequently $\le$ is a total order (trichotomy plus transitivity from closure under sums), compatible with addition (translation preserves the difference) and with multiplication by positives: $\mathbb{R}$ is a totally ordered field. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, L4] ∎
    35	
    36	## Statement
    37	
    38	The Cauchy-sequence reals $\mathbb{R}_C$ have the **least-upper-bound property**:
    39	every nonempty $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}_C$ that is bounded above has a least upper
    40	bound $\sup S \in \mathbb{R}_C$. Hence, together with [[thm-reals-ordered-field]],
    41	$\mathbb{R}_C$ is a **complete ordered field** ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
    42	
    43	## Facts & Assumptions
    44	
    45	**Given:** A nonempty set $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}_C$ bounded above by $U \in \mathbb{R}_C$.
